#: ../../../guide/introduction.md:64
msgid ""
"Partitioning splits a tensor into disjoint regions with a fixed tile shape, "
"such as `partition([4, 4])` for a 2D tensor. Each tile block receives one "
"partition element, which is how cuTile Rust gives the kernel mutable access "
"to one region at a time while keeping writes non-overlapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/introduction.md:66
msgid ""
"The borrow-based form (`&Tensor`, `Partition<&mut Tensor>`) lets you pass "
"tensors without moving them. The kernel writes through the borrow — no "
"`unpartition()` or return capture needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/introduction.md:68
msgid "The simplest launch pattern borrows everything:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/introduction.md:70
msgid ""
"let x = api::ones::<f32>(&[32, 32]).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
"let y = api::ones::<f32>(&[32, 32]).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
"let mut z = api::zeros::<f32>(&[32, 32]).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
"\n"
"// Borrow-based: z is written in place.\n"
"let _ = add((&mut z).partition([4, 4]), &x, &y).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/introduction.md:79
msgid ""
"The launcher also accepts lazy `DeviceOp` arguments — everything stays lazy "
"until `.sync()` or `.await`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/introduction.md:81
msgid ""
"let z = api::zeros(&[32, 32]).partition([4, 4]);\n"
"let x = api::ones::<f32>(&[32, 32]);\n"
"let y = api::ones::<f32>(&[32, 32]);\n"
"\n"
"let (_z, _x, _y) = add(z, x, y).sync()?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/introduction.md:89
msgid "For chaining, use `.then()` to compose operations on the same stream:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/introduction.md:91
msgid ""
"let result = allocate()\n"
"    .then(|buf| fill_kernel(buf))\n"
"    .then(|buf| process_kernel(buf))\n"
"    .sync()?;\n"
msgstr ""

